// Family privacy information. Availability and retention are specific to each surface;
// a developing managed App is not a live service promise. Operator values stay build-only.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SectionHead from '../components/SectionHead.astro';
import LegalOperator from '../components/LegalOperator.astro';
import { LIVE_PLAYGROUND_WORKER, docPaths, paths } from '../data/site';
---
<Base title="Privacy and data" description="Data processing, browser storage and privacy choices for the diffdevil website, Lens Studio, extension and developing managed App." current="privacy" noindex>
  <section class="section section-first">
    <article class="wrap legal">
      <SectionHead id="privacy" as="h1" title="Privacy and data" />
      <p class="lede">The website, Lens Studio and browser extension have different data boundaries. The managed GitHub App is still being prepared for public access; its section below explains that distinction.</p>
      <p class="small">Last updated: 24 October 2026.</p>
      <h2 class="h3" id="controller">Controller and contact</h2>
      <LegalOperator contact="privacy" />
      <p class="small">This operator is responsible for the processing described for our own services. A person or organisation running a self-hosted diffdevil service is responsible for that deployment. GitHub, your browser provider and other sites also process data under their own notices.</p>

      <h2 class="h3" id="website">Website, manual and hosting</h2>
      <p class="small">The website and manual have no visitor accounts, advertising analytics or tracking pixels. Fonts, scripts and search indexes are served with the site. Search terms are evaluated in your browser, without sending the terms to a search service. Downloading an agent Skill or setup file is an ordinary HTTP request, not agent identification or enrolment.</p>
      <p class="small">Cloudflare delivers and protects the sites and public API. It processes connection and request information, including IP address, time, requested URL, browser/request headers, response status and traffic/security information. A URL can include query parameters. These data are needed to return the requested resource, diagnose failures and protect availability; refusing the necessary connection data prevents delivery. For our delivery and security processing we rely on Article 6(1)(f) GDPR: our legitimate interests in providing a reliable public service and preventing abuse.</p>
      <p class="small">We do not turn these requests into visitor profiles or a product analysis-history database. Operational logs and Cloudflare's own network/security data are separate. Cloudflare documents three-day retention for Workers Free logs and seven days for Workers Paid logs when that logging service is enabled; this is not a retention promise for all provider data. Its <a class="link-quiet" href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">privacy policy</a> describes retention of its own data by service, security and legal purpose. Evidence needed for a particular incident or legal claim is kept for that purpose until it is resolved or an applicable legal retention duty ends.</p>

      <h2 class="h3" id="browser-storage">Browser storage and choices</h2>
      <p class="small">Your requested theme is saved in local storage under <code class="code-inline">diffdevil.theme</code> and <code class="code-inline">diffdevil.theme.next</code>. There is no automatic expiry; changing the preference replaces it, and clearing this site's storage removes it. A theme parameter carries the choice between the website and manual and is removed from the destination URL after arrival. It can still be part of the initial request.</p>
      <p class="small">Lens Studio keeps a saved lesson's last checked public GitHub revision in session storage to avoid repeating its freshness check during that browser session. Loaded lesson/report data are also held in page memory. Its lifetime follows your browser's page-session and session-restoration behavior; clearing site data removes it. Ordinary browser HTTP caches and history are managed by your browser.</p>
      <p class="small">These operations support the theme or analysis service you request. Necessary device storage/access is based on § 25(2)(2) TDDDG; where it involves personal data, the separate GDPR basis is Article 6(1)(f), our interest in delivering those requested functions. We do not use this storage for advertising or cross-site tracking. You can clear or block it in your browser; preferences or session reuse may then stop working. The site has no optional analytics or advertising-storage operation requiring a consent banner.</p>

      <h2 class="h3" id="lens-studio">Lens Studio and the public API</h2>
      <p class="small">Saved lessons load published fixture files from the site and are evaluated in your browser. Opening a lesson also checks its public PR's current revision through the API, unless that session already has the check. Editing a policy runs locally. A live public-PR analysis sends the PR URL to <code class="code-inline">{LIVE_PLAYGROUND_WORKER}</code>; the API reads public GitHub metadata and diff evidence and returns the result. It does not ask for a GitHub token, access private repositories or write to a repository.</p>
      <p class="small">The PR URL travels in an API request query. Your policy and selected comparison can also travel in the page URL for sharing, reloading or carrying the policy to the App explanation page. Anyone given that URL can read its contents; browser history, hosting request logs and copied links may retain it. Do not put secrets, private configuration or unnecessary personal information in a policy or link. Encoding a policy in a URL does not make it confidential.</p>
      <p class="small">The API processes public repository/PR identities, comparison references, filenames, measurements and source/diff content needed for the requested calculation. Source is transient during acquisition and calculation. The API has no persistent visitor analysis history or implemented result cache. Its responses use <code class="code-inline">no-store</code>; that does not erase your browser history, exported results or provider logs. GitHub's shared unauthenticated allowance and input/size limits can prevent acquisition. Requests may fail when those limits are reached.</p>
      <p class="small">Our basis is Article 6(1)(f) GDPR: providing the analysis the visitor requests, with public input and bounded acquisition. GitHub supplies the public repository information, which can contain personal data about contributors even when the visitor did not supply it directly. Cloudflare processes the request and response; GitHub receives our acquisition requests under its own service terms. Providing a valid public PR URL is necessary for live analysis; saved lessons remain an alternative. The service produces measurements, not decisions about people with legal or similarly significant effects.</p>

      <h2 class="h3" id="extension">Browser extension</h2>
      <p class="small">When you use the extension on a GitHub pull request, it reads the relevant page and obtains diff and trusted policy evidence from GitHub. Private-repository acquisition uses your existing signed-in GitHub session; a separate public API path uses unauthenticated reads. Analysis runs in the extension's local worker. The extension does not upload repository source or analysis to Wolfsblvt Works or the public Playground and has no telemetry or analytics endpoint. GitHub still receives the requests to its resources.</p>
      <p class="small">A rebuildable local cache retains normalized reports, filenames/comparison metadata, trusted policy/template text and comparison pointers. Raw patch bodies are not persisted there. The cache survives browser restarts, has no time-based expiry, and evicts older entries to stay within its size budget: 16 MiB by default, adjustable from 4 to 128 MiB. Cache invalidation, the extension's clear controls and uninstall can remove it. It is not independently encrypted by diffdevil; protect access to your browser profile. Pausing a repository stops work but does not erase cached data or already-rendered page content.</p>
      <p class="small">Small preferences use Chrome synchronized storage and may travel through your browser account to other signed-in browsers when synchronization is enabled. Advanced policy text, repository overrides and paused-repository choices are stored locally. A separate record of the comparison most recently analysed uses extension session storage. A bounded local diagnostic list keeps recent technical event codes and context; a support snapshot is copied only when you choose to share it. Review any exported settings before sending them.</p>
      <p class="small">This storage and access support the extension functions you request (§ 25(2)(2) TDDDG); to the extent we determine processing of personal data for those functions, Article 6(1)(f) GDPR supports that purpose. We do not receive the local dataset merely because the extension is installed. Disabling or uninstalling the extension ends its operation. See the <a class="link-quiet" href={docPaths.extensionPrivacy}>extension privacy information</a> and <a class="link-quiet" href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/extensions/reference/api/storage">Chrome storage documentation</a> for its controls and synchronization behavior. Store availability is shown on the <a class="link-quiet" href={paths.extension}>extension page</a>.</p>

      <h2 class="h3" id="managed-app">Managed GitHub App</h2>
      <p class="small">The managed App and signed-in dashboard are being prepared for public access. This website does not open a paid subscription or qualify a live customer service. The following describes the current product boundary; the actual hosted offer must provide its service-specific retention, provider and contractual information before customer admission. The <a class="link-quiet" href={docPaths.privacyAndData}>product data documentation</a> explains the engineering contract.</p>
      <ul class="small">
        <li><strong>Operation and access.</strong> GitHub account, installation and repository data establish sign-in, current access and explicitly enabled repository operation. Protected settings can include policies, exclusions, labels, templates and administrator permissions. Administrative records explain changes; they are separate from contributor analytics.</li>
        <li><strong>Optional history.</strong> Explicitly enabled history can retain repository names and IDs, PR/comparison references, lifecycle times, current/previous relative file paths, numerical measurements, coverage and bounded policy/effect results. These are protected, linkable metadata, not anonymous data. Policy exclusion changes interpretation; it does not necessarily exclude permitted measurements from collection. Source and diff content may be acquired transiently in memory to calculate measurements. Source bodies, patches, full diffs, raw webhooks, PR/commit prose and review/comment bodies are excluded from analytical retention. Separately supplied configuration and feedback have their own purposes.</li>
        <li><strong>Controls and lifetimes.</strong> Installation, execution and history enablement are separate. Stopping collection leaves retained history; deletion and export are distinct controls. Current access is checked for reads, including copied file links. Operational recovery uses a seven-day boundary. History, account/configuration, audit and backup/archive records have separate policies; an internal runtime expiry default is not a customer retention promise. Exported copies already delivered cannot be recalled.</li>
        <li><strong>GitHub authorization.</strong> Protected session cookies support sign-in; authorization material is sealed separately. Logout ends that browser session. A grant may remain for another live session, pending sign-in or an enabled, funded organisation connection that still needs to check GitHub authority. Disconnecting that service connection clears the local grant and its sessions; it does not cancel payment or unbind funding. Deliberate reconnect is separate from ordinary sign-in.</li>
        <li><strong>Works and feedback.</strong> A connected Works account supplies commercial standing needed to apply benefits, not payment instruments or invoices. Optional participation-survey answers and free text stay in diffdevil; Works receives completion facts, not answers or repository context. Deleting answers preserves the completion identity and time associated with the reward. Closing the diffdevil account deletes its participation records; Works keeps its own commercial records under its terms.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="small">A future public analytical view requires an explicit publication choice for an eligible public repository; a public GitHub repository is not automatic permission to publish its analytics. Private repository history remains protected. Before hosted admission, the offer must settle controller/processor roles, the basis and notice for affected repository participants, actual history/audit/backup retention, and working export, deletion and authorization journeys. This development description does not substitute for those disclosures or an applicable processing agreement.</p>

      <h2 class="h3" id="self-hosting">CLI, Actions, library and self-hosting</h2>
      <p class="small">Running the CLI, library, GitHub Actions or a Community/self-hosted deployment does not by itself send results to Wolfsblvt Works. Your selected runtime, GitHub workflow, integrations and hosting determine who receives data and how long it remains. The deployment operator chooses its retention and resource limits and must supply its own privacy information. Our hosted notice cannot describe another operator's logs, backups or configuration.</p>

      <h2 class="h3" id="contact-support">Contact and support</h2>
      <p class="small">When you email us or use the linked Works contact route, we process your contact details, message and evidence you choose to send to answer the request. Only information needed to understand and reply is required; without a return address we may be unable to answer. The legal basis is Article 6(1)(b) GDPR for your contractual or pre-contractual request, otherwise Article 6(1)(f), our interest in handling inquiries and resolving faults. An applicable legal duty can require retention under Article 6(1)(c).</p>
      <p class="small">The operator and the mail/contact service providers handling that channel receive the message. The Works form supplies its own channel-specific privacy information. We keep correspondence while handling the request and any connected dispute or legal retention duty, then delete it when those purposes end. Avoid sending credentials, private code or full browser profiles. A GitHub issue is public: use the private contact or <a class="link-quiet" href={paths.security}>security reporting route</a> when a report contains sensitive material. Choosing to send a diagnostic snapshot makes it part of that support correspondence; local diagnostics are not automatically collected.</p>

      <h2 class="h3" id="providers-transfers">Providers and international processing</h2>
      <p class="small">Cloudflare, Inc. delivers and protects our public sites/API; GitHub provides repository and platform services. They may process data in the United States and other countries where they and their subprocessors operate. Cloudflare's <a class="link-quiet" href="https://www.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-customer-dpa/">data-processing terms</a> describe processor safeguards and standard contractual clauses; its <a class="link-quiet" href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">privacy policy</a> also describes its own controller processing and applicable Data Privacy Framework commitments. GitHub's <a class="link-quiet" href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">privacy statement</a> identifies its roles, transfer safeguards and retention criteria. The applicable adequacy framework or contractual safeguards depend on the recipient and transfer. You can obtain the published safeguards through those links or request information from our privacy contact. We do not promise EU-only processing.</p>

      <h2 class="h3" id="rights">Your rights</h2>
      <p class="small">Subject to the applicable conditions, you have rights of access, rectification, erasure, restriction and data portability. Where processing relies on consent, you may withdraw it at any time without affecting earlier lawful processing. Contact the controller above to exercise your rights, including for information obtained from GitHub. You may complain to a data-protection supervisory authority, particularly in the EU Member State where you live, work or consider an infringement occurred; you do not have to contact us first.</p>
      <h2 class="h3" id="objection">Your right to object</h2>
      <p class="small">You may object to processing based on Article 6(1)(f) GDPR on grounds relating to your particular situation. We then stop that processing unless we demonstrate compelling legitimate grounds overriding your interests, rights and freedoms, or need it for legal claims. Use the privacy contact above. We do not use these services for direct-marketing profiles.</p>
      <p class="small">Legal notice: <a class="link-quiet" href={paths.impressum}>Impressum</a>. Security reports: <a class="link-quiet" href={paths.security}>Security</a>.</p>
    </article>
  </section>
</Base>
